 "deepDive": {
  "domain": "Foreign relations",
  "title": "After the midterms: three partnerships, all on hold",
  "body": "Three days after Democrats won both chambers of Congress, none of the three relationships has formally changed. What has shifted is the timing, and each capital's sense of leverage.\n\n**US–Poland.** Warsaw's priority is still a permanent US base. On Nov 4, Deputy Defence Minister Sobkowiak-Czarnecka said a decision rests with Trump and should come within a few weeks. Her remarks followed talks with a Pentagon team led by Elbridge Colby in Kraków ([PAP/Portal Samorządowy](https://www.portalsamorzadowy.pl/polityka-i-spoleczenstwo/sobkowiak-czarnecka-sprawa-stalej-amerykanskiej-bazy-w-polsce-powinna-sie-rozstrzygnac-w-ciagu-kilku-tygodni,682962.html)). Kosiniak-Kamysz plans to see Hegseth at the Pentagon early next week. Hegseth himself, though, faces an impeachment threat from Republican Thomas Massie ([RMF24](https://www.rmf24.pl/fakty/swiat/news-pete-hegseth-i-marco-rubio-odchodza-od-trumpa-bialy-dom-rozw,nIdn,1025048)).\n\nThe base is also a fight at home. President Nawrocki's aides say the window is closing and the government is slow. PiS's Błaszczak says the midterms have killed the project, while the government calls it a shared priority. On Ukraine, Warsaw's fear is being sidelined. Sikorski warned against deals made over eastern Europeans' heads as US-run talks resume in Abu Dhabi ([Euronews](https://www.euronews.com/2026/11/06/poland-warns-allies-dont-negotiate-with-russia-over-eastern-europes-head)).\n\n**US–India.** The interim trade deal is still unsigned, and Indian goods pay a 10% Section 301 duty. Officials note that Congress does not need to approve the deal. Analysts are split on whether a weakened Trump will move faster. Attention now turns to Manila in mid-November, where Trump is due at the ASEAN summit and a Quad leaders' meeting is planned. A Modi–Trump bilateral is **not confirmed**. On visas, H-1B limits rest on executive rules, so courts rather than the new Congress are the likelier route to change.\n\n**Poland–India.** This is the calmest of the three. After PM Tusk's October visit and joint statement, Commerce Minister Jitin Prasada brought 12 Indian firms to Warsaw this week, but no deals were signed. The classified-information agreement, which defence-industry cooperation depends on, is still unsigned.\n\n**Next:** the Hegseth meeting, Trump's base decision and Manila.",
